"hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:"
2837,"**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Bashshar, narrated to us Abu Ahmad az-Zubayri, narrated to us Sufyan, from Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah, from Zayd ibn 'Ali, from his father, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi', from 'Ali (RA), who said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood at Arafah, then he departed when the sun had set, and he made Usamah ibn Zayd ride behind him.""

Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: ""Ja'far ibn Muhammad informed me, from his father, from Jabir, with a similar narration."""
2838,"**
Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu 'Amir who said: narrated to us Zam'ah, from Salamah — and he is Ibn Wahraam —, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbaas (RA) who said:
""During the pre-Islamic era (Jahiliyyah), the people would stand at 'Arafah until the sun was directly above the mountains, resembling turbans upon the heads of men, then they would depart. They would then stand at Muzdalifah until the sun rose, and when it was directly above the mountains, resembling turbans upon the heads of men, they would depart. So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delayed the departure from 'Arafah until the sun had set. He then prayed Fajr at Muzdalifah when the dawn broke. He then departed when everything was fully illuminated in the later part of the time, before the sun rose.""

Abu Bakr said: ""I absolve myself from any responsibility regarding the narration of Zam'ah ibn Saalih."""
